Track 3: Who Promised We Would Land?
- Story & Context: Four decades after the original Deck 14 uprising was violently suppressed, salvage teams roaming the ship's frozen outer ring discovered that Kaelen’s distorted lead sequence had burned permanently into the hull's titanium acoustic memory. The fourth-generation laborers had built an entire mythos around the repeating metallic resonance, gathering at ship-night to lock boots together on the vibrating floorplates and stomp in unison with the spectral sub-bass. When ship marshals attempted to cut the structural struts to silence the anthem, the entire labor force laid down their tools, letting the hydroponic temperatures plummet while their thousands of voices echoed the ancient refrain down the ventilation corridors: "You bought our tombs on credit, but our fury rides for free." The administration ultimately backed down, leaving the phantom lead line to howl through the metal all the way to Proxima Centauri.
